    summary: 'The application layer of an LLM system is almost always a CPython process: the interpreter compiles source to bytecode and executes it in an evaluation loop, manages objects with reference counting backed by a cyclic garbage collector, and allocates small objects from its own arenas on top of malloc. Everything else in the stack (PyTorch, the CUDA runtime, NCCL) is loaded into this process as shared libraries and called through C extensions.',
    purpose: 'Hosts the model code, the server and the engine control loop, and owns the address space into which GPU libraries, pinned buffers and mapped weight files are attached.',
    mechanics: 'Source is compiled to bytecode (cached as `.pyc`) and executed by the `ceval` loop, one bytecode at a time, with a specializing adaptive interpreter in recent versions (3.11+) that inlines common cases. Every object carries a reference count that is incremented and decremented on each binding; an object is freed immediately when the count reaches zero, so most memory is reclaimed deterministically without a collector pass. Reference cycles are found by a generational cyclic GC that runs when allocation thresholds are crossed and can pause the thread for milliseconds when many container objects are live. Small objects (up to 512 bytes) come from `pymalloc` pools inside 256 KB or 1 MB arenas; larger objects and all tensor storage go to the system allocator or, for CUDA tensors, to the PyTorch caching allocator. The process holds one CUDA primary context per device it touches, and its virtual address space includes the mapped driver, library code, mmap-ed weight files and any pinned host buffers, so RSS and mapped size can differ by tens of gigabytes. A serving deployment commonly runs one Python process per GPU (or per tensor-parallel group) because the GIL prevents a single process from driving many GPUs with Python-level parallelism, and because a crash or CUDA error in one process then only takes down one device.',
