    summary: 'CPython serializes bytecode execution with a single mutex per interpreter: only the thread holding the GIL runs Python code, and the interpreter forces a handoff after a switch interval (5 ms by default) when other threads are waiting. C extensions release the GIL around long-running native work and blocking system calls, so PyTorch kernels, NCCL calls and socket reads run concurrently with Python threads. CPU-bound pure-Python threads therefore do not scale across cores; processes or native code do.',
    purpose: 'Protects reference counts and interpreter state without fine-grained locking; the design choice that makes single-threaded CPython fast and multi-threaded CPython non-parallel.',
    mechanics: 'A thread acquires the GIL before executing bytecode and drops it when it blocks in a call marked with `Py_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS`, which PyTorch does around most operator dispatch and CUDA calls and which the standard library does around I/O. A waiting thread sets a request flag after `sys.getswitchinterval()` seconds; the holder checks the flag at the evaluation-loop boundary and yields, then the OS scheduler decides who runs next, so handoff latency is the switch interval plus scheduling delay. Two CPU-bound Python threads run no faster than one and can run slower because of lock contention and cache migration (the convoy effect). Native work is different: a thread that has released the GIL during a 2 ms CUDA kernel launch sequence lets another thread run scheduling logic in Python. Multi-process designs avoid the lock entirely at the cost of IPC and duplicated memory. PEP 703 free-threaded builds (`python3.13t` and later) remove the GIL behind a build flag with per-object locks and biased reference counting; they are optional and experimental, carry a single-thread overhead and require extensions to declare compatibility, so production ML stacks commonly still run the default GIL build. Sub-interpreters (PEP 684) give each interpreter its own GIL but do not share Python objects.',
