    summary: 'An event loop multiplexes many in-flight requests on one thread: coroutines run until they await something, and the loop resumes them when the underlying socket or future becomes ready, using `epoll` (Linux) under the hood. This is concurrency (many requests interleaved), not parallelism (one core executing Python at any time). LLM servers are async because a streaming response is a long-lived connection that mostly waits, and thousands of them cost little on one thread.',
    purpose: 'Keeps thousands of open streaming connections and per-request state machines cheap, and lets request handling overlap with waiting on the engine and the network.',
    mechanics: 'The loop calls `epoll_wait` (or `kqueue`/IOCP) for ready file descriptors, then runs each ready callback or coroutine step to completion before checking again. A coroutine only yields at `await`, so any synchronous work inside it (JSON parsing of a large body, tokenizing a long prompt, a blocking `time.sleep`, a synchronous `.result()`) blocks every other connection for its duration. uvloop replaces the default loop with a libuv-based implementation that is commonly 2-4× faster for socket-heavy paths. Token streaming maps naturally onto async generators: the engine pushes each new token into a per-request queue and the handler awaits the queue and writes an SSE chunk. Blocking CPU work is moved off the loop with `run_in_executor` (a thread pool, still GIL-bound) or into another process. Because the loop is one thread, its scheduling delay is added to every response: if one handler holds the loop for 20 ms, every concurrent stream sees 20 ms of extra latency, which is why loop blocking appears as p99 rather than mean latency. Backpressure is explicit: bounded queues and `await writer.drain()` stop a slow client from buffering unboundedly.',
