    mechanics: 'A call such as `y = x @ w` enters a C++ binding, unpacks arguments, resolves the op and dispatches to a kernel; the kernel launch is asynchronous, so Python moves on while the GPU is still working, and the wrapper returned holds device pointers rather than data. With gradients enabled, autograd records a node (`grad_fn`) per op with references to saved tensors, adding allocation and bookkeeping to each call and keeping activations alive until backward. `torch.no_grad()` skips the graph; `torch.inference_mode()` additionally disables version counters and view tracking for a further reduction. Views (`transpose`, slicing) create new wrappers on the same storage without a kernel, but they force copies later in ops that need contiguous inputs. Because launches are asynchronous, the CPU can run ahead of the GPU until a synchronizing call (`.item()`, `.cpu()`, `torch.cuda.synchronize()`, printing a tensor) drains the queue; a synchronization in the middle of a step converts the run-ahead margin into GPU idle time. A decode step of a small model may issue hundreds of kernels that each run for tens of microseconds; when host time per op exceeds device time per op, the GPU starves and the timeline shows gaps between kernels. The fixes are fewer, larger ops (fusion), removing Python from the hot path (torch.compile, CUDA graphs) or batching more work per op.',
