    summary: 'Everything performance-critical in the application layer is native code reached through a binding layer: PyTorch itself is a C++ library exposed via pybind11-style bindings, and custom kernels (attention, quantized GEMM, sampling) are shipped as extension modules that register operators. The binding converts Python arguments to C++ types, typically releases the GIL, calls into CUDA, and wraps the result. ABI compatibility between the extension, the PyTorch build and the CUDA toolkit is what makes wheels fragile.',
