    summary: 'An inference engine is a process that owns one GPU (or a tensor-parallel group) and runs a fixed loop: pick the next batch from waiting and running requests, run one forward step, sample the next token for each sequence, and push outputs to whoever is streaming them. Modern engines (vLLM is the reference example here) split this loop into its own process, connected to the API server by a message channel, so that HTTP handling, tokenization and detokenization never share a GIL or an event loop with the step loop.',
    purpose: 'Keeps the GPU continuously busy by making the host-side step as short and predictable as possible and by isolating it from everything else the application does.',
    mechanics: 'Each iteration the scheduler reads the queues and produces a batch plan under token and KV-block budgets; the model runner builds the input tensors (token ids, positions, block tables, sampling metadata), runs the forward pass (for decode commonly by replaying a CUDA graph captured for that batch size), and the sampler applies logit processors and picks tokens on the GPU. The chosen ids are copied to the host (one small D2H per step), appended to sequences, checked against stop conditions, and sent back to the front end, where detokenization turns ids into text incrementally. Overlap matters: engines run the scheduler for step N+1 while the GPU executes step N, and copy sampled ids asynchronously, so the loop’s critical path is roughly `max(host step work, GPU step time)` rather than their sum. The API process and the engine process communicate over a socket or ZeroMQ channel with serialized messages (msgpack or pickle); the round trip adds tens to hundreds of microseconds per step, small relative to a typical decode step of several milliseconds. In tensor-parallel deployments the engine process holds rank 0 and broadcasts each step’s inputs to worker processes; all ranks execute the same schedule and exchange partial results with NCCL inside the forward. The engine preallocates the KV pool at startup (weights plus a memory-utilization target) so no allocation happens in the loop. A step that stalls (GC pause, a synchronizing call, a slow IPC read) is directly visible as an idle gap on the GPU.',
