    keywords: ['pickle', 'protobuf', 'msgpack', 'ZeroMQ', 'Unix socket', 'shm', 'memfd', 'torch.multiprocessing', 'cudaIpcGetMemHandle', 'DLPack', 'Arrow'],
    summary: 'Processes exchange two kinds of data: small structured control messages and large tensors. Control messages are serialized (pickle for Python objects, protobuf for gRPC, msgpack for speed) and sent over pipes, Unix sockets or ZeroMQ. Tensors should not be serialized at all: CPU tensors are placed in shared memory and only a descriptor is sent, and device tensors are shared through CUDA IPC handles that map the same allocation into another process. Copying a tensor through a socket costs memory bandwidth and time proportional to its size; sharing a handle costs microseconds regardless of size.',
    purpose: 'Moves work and results between the API server, the engine and its workers without copying bulk data through the interpreter.',
    mechanics: 'Pickle walks the object graph and produces a byte stream; it is general but slow (hundreds of megabytes per second) and unsafe with untrusted input, which is why engines use it only on internal channels. msgpack and protobuf encode flat records at a few gigabytes per second and with a fixed schema. Transport: a Unix domain socket round trip costs of the order of 10-30 µs, dominated by two syscalls and a context switch on each side, and ZeroMQ adds framing and queueing but keeps the same order of magnitude. For CPU tensors, `torch.multiprocessing` moves the storage into a shared-memory file (`/dev/shm` or memfd) on first send, and later sends carry only a file descriptor and offset; the receiving process mmaps it and sees the same pages, which is zero-copy in the sense that no bytes are duplicated, though the first placement into shared memory is itself a copy. For device tensors, `cudaIpcGetMemHandle` exports an allocation and `cudaIpcOpenMemHandle` maps it into the peer process’s context, subject to same-device or peer-access constraints; NCCL uses the same mechanism internally for intra-node transport. DLPack passes tensors between frameworks in the same process by pointer without copying. Serialization of the per-step batch metadata (hundreds of request ids, block tables) is a measurable part of an engine step and is why engines minimize what crosses the boundary and reuse buffers.',
    input: 'Python objects and tensors to be sent between processes.',
    output: 'Bytes on a channel, or descriptors (fd + offset, CUDA IPC handle) that map shared memory in the receiver.',
