    summary: 'Loading a model moves tens to hundreds of gigabytes from storage into GPU memory, through the host page cache and a host-to-device copy. safetensors makes the host side cheap: the file is a header plus raw tensor bytes and is memory-mapped, so tensors are materialized lazily without a deserialization pass. The bound is then the slowest link: disk or network read bandwidth, the PCIe copy, and any per-tensor overhead; a 70B-class model in BF16 (about 140 GB) at roughly 25 GB/s of effective H2D bandwidth over PCIe 4.0 x16 takes about a minute for the copy alone, per node, before anything else.',
    purpose: 'Gets weights resident in HBM in the right sharded layout for the parallelism scheme, as fast as the storage and PCIe path allow.',
    mechanics: 'A safetensors file is mmap-ed; the page cache backs it, so the first read of each region is a disk or network read and repeated loads on the same node hit cache at memory speed. Each tensor is sliced from the mapping (no copy in host memory), converted if the dtype differs, and copied to the device; with pageable source memory each copy stages through a driver-owned pinned buffer, so throughput drops to roughly half of pinned and cannot overlap fully. Fast loaders instead allocate a few pinned staging buffers, read tensor bytes into them (possibly with multiple threads that release the GIL) and issue `cudaMemcpyAsync` on a copy stream, overlapping disk reads with PCIe copies. Tensor parallelism means each rank loads only its shard of each weight (`[out/TP, in]` or `[out, in/TP]` slices), which multiplies aggregate load bandwidth by the number of ranks but also multiplies concurrent reads of the same files. Quantized checkpoints and on-the-fly quantization change the byte count and add a compute pass. Cold starts add container image pull, Python imports (seconds), CUDA context creation, CUDA graph capture and torch.compile warm-up on top of the copy; the copy is often the largest term when the page cache is cold and the storage is network-attached at a few GB/s. GPUDirect Storage can move bytes from NVMe to HBM without the host bounce buffer where supported.',
