    summary: 'Profiling at the application layer answers one question first: is the GPU waiting on the host, or is the host waiting on the GPU? A timeline that aligns Python frames, CUDA API calls and kernel executions shows this directly as gaps between kernels. py-spy samples Python stacks without instrumentation, torch.profiler records ops and kernels together, and nsys captures the CUDA runtime, driver, OS threads and NVTX ranges across the whole process.',
    purpose: 'Locates the bottleneck across layers so that optimization effort goes to the layer that bounds throughput or latency.',
    mechanics: 'py-spy attaches to a running process, reads interpreter state from outside (no code changes, low overhead) and produces top-style output, flame graphs or dumps; `--gil` shows which thread holds the lock and `--native` includes C frames. torch.profiler (Kineto underneath) records each ATen op with its CPU duration and correlates it to the CUDA kernels it launched, exporting a Chrome trace where a GPU row full of short kernels separated by gaps means launch-bound execution and a GPU row that is solid while the CPU row idles means GPU-bound. nsys records CUDA runtime and driver calls, kernels, memcpys, OS thread states and NVTX ranges (`torch.cuda.nvtx.range` or `nvtx` annotations around scheduler, forward, sample) so that an engine step can be read as a sequence of named phases with their host and device durations. Diagnosis rules: if kernel time per step is much smaller than step wall time, the host is the bottleneck (Python overhead, IPC, synchronization, GC); if the GPU is busy but achieved bandwidth or FLOP/s is far below peak, the kernels themselves need work (Nsight Compute); if `cudaStreamSynchronize` or `cudaMemcpy` dominate the CUDA API row, a synchronizing call is preventing run-ahead. Sampling profilers perturb little; tracing profilers add overhead per op and should be run on a few steps after warm-up, since the first steps include allocator growth, cuDNN autotuning, JIT and graph capture.',
